00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:38.490
So in a sense,
TensorFlow is really

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:45.310
democratizing machine
learning for a lot of us.

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:50.890
And machine learning engine
is a fully managed service

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.800
where you can run
TensorFlow training

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:59.740
and prediction at scale on
Google's cloud infrastructure.

00:03:59.740 --> 00:04:04.360
And today, we just announced
the general availability

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:07.080
of machine learning engine.

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.880
And using ML Engine,
what you can do

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:13.990
is write your powerful
machine learning algorithm

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.630
using TensorFlow,
run the training

00:04:16.630 --> 00:04:18.130
with terabytes of data--

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.780
it's not just gigabytes,
terabytes of real big data--

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.590
and deploy the model
with just one command,

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:30.670
and serve the prediction
for millions of users,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:36.100
and do that without worrying
about building your machine

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:39.430
learning infrastructure
or maintaining that,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:41.450
which takes away the time.

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:44.830
So we'd like you
to basically focus

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:48.610
on building great machine
learning applications

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.820
instead of managing
your infrastructure.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.750
So using these tools,
using machine learning

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.030
to transform your business
is not a topic of future,

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.940
but it's the topic of today.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:13.360
And in this session, I'd like to
share seven stories from Google

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:18.320
Cloud customers who
have taken advantage

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.120
of machine learning to apply
that to their core business

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.140
problems.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.380
And some of them had this
problem for a very, very long

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:28.205
time, like decades.

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.750
So hopefully, you can get
some ideas and inspirations

00:05:34.750 --> 00:05:39.970
about how you can use machine
learning to solve your business

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.390
problems or improve your
apps, improve your customer

00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:44.450
experience.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.930
So our first story is from AXA.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:55.310
They are one of the largest
global insurance firms.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:59.420
And they used machine
learning to solve

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:02.270
a problem for one of
their core business, which

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:04.590
is car insurance.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.000
So here's a little bit
of an interesting number

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:10.250
for car insurance.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:17.390
So out of all insured drivers,
about 7% to 10% of drivers

00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:19.910
get into an accident.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:23.270
And most of these
accidents are minor.

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:26.210
It requires just a small fix-up.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:33.260
But 1% of all these
accidents is large.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:38.190
And that requires over
$10,000 of insurance payment.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:42.350
And they call it large-loss
car accident cases.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:45.830
And it is very important
for an insurance company

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:48.380
to understand the risk
of somebody getting

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.140
into such large car accidents.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:58.170
So what they did was to
take the information that

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:01.350
typically they use to
evaluate these risks--

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.750
there are 70 features they used.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:09.240
There are things like age range
of the driver, the region,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.620
the information about
their insurance policy--

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.350
and feed them into
a neural network

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:21.000
that has three hidden
layers and ReLU activation.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:27.150
And they got the amazing
result of 78% accuracy, which

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.600
was a huge jump from
their previous method

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.670
that was using
random forest, which

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:37.090
is another machine
learning algorithm.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:42.420
And with this kind of
accuracy, this basically

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:46.260
enables better
pricing for customers.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:49.890
Also, it opens up the
possibility of better services,

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:53.400
like real-time insurance
pricing, which also benefits

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:56.350
the customer experience.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:08:01.170
So let's take a look
at the data in action

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.420
in the neural network.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:08.130
So they created this
very cool visualization

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:14.910
to actually showcase the neural
network training in action.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.620
So on the right-hand
side, there are

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.800
lots of features fed
into the neural network.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.370
And the lines are the connection
inside the neural network.

00:08:28.370 --> 00:08:30.710
The boxes are the neurons.

00:08:30.710 --> 00:08:32.630
And the training started.

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:37.460
And the color orange and blue
means positive and negative

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:38.100
values.

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:40.730
And you can see the
thickness representing

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.429
the weight of the connection.

00:08:43.429 --> 00:08:45.180
And training goes on.

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:47.000
And on the right-hand
side, you can

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.290
see the graph of test accuracy.

00:08:50.290 --> 00:08:54.230
So that is the accuracy
score for the test data set

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:57.000
that is held out from
the training data set.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.190
It is very important in machine
learning to segregate these two

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:02.270
data sets.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.690
And you can see the
training is converging.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:08.780
And after some
amount of time, it

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:11.085
gets to quite high number of 78.

00:09:13.805 --> 00:09:19.600
So that was a very
cool visualization.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.400
So that was an example
from insurance industry.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.810
Our next story is from
Airbus Defence and Space.

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:31.750
So many of you might
know the name Airbus.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.500
And Airbus Defense and Space
is a world-leading company

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:39.020
in the space industry.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:42.470
So they had this
problem to detect clouds

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:46.040
in satellite imagery
for 20 years.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:49.220
So they are the experts
in this problem,

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.660
and they have been
devising the solution

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:54.710
to solve this
problem for 20 years.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:56.910
And here's one example image--

00:09:56.910 --> 00:10:00.110
so snow-capped mountains,
and there are clouds.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:04.920
And it's really hard to
see, but here is the answer.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.360
So if you were able to tell
all the pieces of clouds,

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:09.680
then congratulations.

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.350
You have very,
very good eyesight.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:16.580
So it's very, hard
even for humans.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:23.690
And the process naturally
involves a lot of manual work

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:27.830
to accurately tag these clouds.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:31.850
And as the amount of data that
come through their pipeline

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:37.480
increases day by day and at the
level of 10,000 images per day,

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:40.520
their manual process
no longer can

00:10:40.520 --> 00:10:44.010
scale to sustain that workload.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:48.290
So what they did was to
create machine learning-based,

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:51.560
automated approach to
tackle this problem.

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:55.730
And they got an amazing result
of reducing the error rate

00:10:55.730 --> 00:11:00.620
from 11% with their
production system

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:05.090
to 3% with the new approach.

00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:10.610
And what's even more amazing is
the speed of their development.

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:13.520
So they got the first
promising result

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:17.761
with their proof of concept
within the first month

00:11:17.761 --> 00:11:18.385
of their trial.

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:24.880
So when you think
about the time frame,

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.340
they had this
problem for 20 years.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:32.230
And one month of intense
project, and they

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:35.170
got a 70% reduction of errors.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:40.300
So you can imagine how joyful
they were to find this result.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.020
And with this new
approach, basically, they

00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:48.370
can use order of
hundreds of hours

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:52.720
per week of human time,
the precious human time,

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:59.430
to even more challenging task
that require human processing.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:03.300
And here is a very brief
summary of their approach.

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:07.430
So they combined multiple
convolutional neural networks

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:10.105
that goes through different--

00:12:10.105 --> 00:12:13.280
the images go through different
kind of image processing

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.730
to capture different
aspects of the image.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:20.390
And they connect these
networks with fully connected

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:21.750
neural network.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:25.610
So this is quite a
huge network to train.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:30.590
So it's very computationally
intensive to train.

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:34.640
So they used ML Engine's
GPU training capability

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:37.670
to accelerate the training.

00:12:37.670 --> 00:12:44.650
And they got the amazing
acceleration of 40 times speed.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:46.390
So that is pretty good.

00:12:46.390 --> 00:12:49.930
And also, they used the
distributed training.

00:12:49.930 --> 00:12:55.270
So in total, they used to
have 50 hours of training time

00:12:55.270 --> 00:12:57.194
on their desktop machine.

00:12:57.194 --> 00:12:58.360
So that's quite a long time.

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.370
You need to wait for a few days.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:06.300
And they reduced the time to
30 minutes on cloud ML Engine.

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:09.840
And with that kind of time,
basically you can start the job

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.640
and go grab some coffee and
do a small chat, come back,

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.250
and then the result
is already there.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.170
And you can iterate on that.

00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.140
So it enables a
very fast iteration

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:28.550
to improve the model quality
in a very short amount of time.

00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:33.890
Also, they used the feature
called HyperTune on ML engine

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:37.990
that basically automates
the hyperparameter tuning.

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:40.340
And hyperparameter
tuning, some of you

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:43.550
may know that it is a
very manual process that

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.650
takes quite a lot of time.

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:50.540
And HyperTune basically
do that on behalf of you

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:54.770
so that you can be more
productive on improving

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:55.470
your model.

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:05.550
And the third story we have
is from Global Fishing Watch.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:09.300
They have a very ambitious
goal of preventing

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.450
overfishing across the globe.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:16.170
And they do that by
providing transparency

00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:19.590
over the fishing activities
happening across the ocean.

00:14:19.590 --> 00:14:22.410
And that's a very
big-scale problem.

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.890
And overfishing is actually
a very severe problem that

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:29.320
is relevant to a lot of us.

00:14:29.320 --> 00:14:31.930
Fish is a very
important food source.

00:14:31.930 --> 00:14:36.250
And also, the resource
is under stress.

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:41.770
The fishing stock across the
ocean is decreasing rapidly.

00:14:41.770 --> 00:14:45.640
And part of the reason why this
problem is so hard to catch

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:50.770
is understanding the
activities on the ocean

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:52.990
takes a lot of
manual monitoring,

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:56.290
and the existing
approach doesn't scale.

00:14:59.340 --> 00:15:02.340
So they created machine
learning-based approach

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:07.930
and enabled watching the
entire surface of the ocean.

00:15:07.930 --> 00:15:11.870
That is more than
140 square miles.

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:13.700
Now, that is a
really big number.

00:15:13.700 --> 00:15:15.350
It's really hard to grasp.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:20.390
So to put that into the
context, it's 37 times

00:15:20.390 --> 00:15:24.560
the entire United States,
which is quite a large area

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.300
to monitor.

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:31.590
And they created the model to
analyze the movement patterns

00:15:31.590 --> 00:15:37.260
of vessels to classify different
kinds of boats and ships,

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:41.340
and then also understand exactly
when and where the fishing

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:44.170
activities are happening.

00:15:44.170 --> 00:15:46.960
And here's a little
illustration of different kinds

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:48.000
of movement patterns.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.910
And as you can see, there's
very distinct movement patterns.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:57.940
And also, there are non-fishing
vessels, like cargo ships.

00:15:57.940 --> 00:16:02.900
So it's important to
distinguish these as well.

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:07.160
And their approach was
convolutional neural network,

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:09.510
and they had quite
a lot of features.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:12.800
So 100,000 features
coming into the network,

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.100
because there are a
lot of time points.

00:16:16.100 --> 00:16:18.950
And each time point
has 11 features.

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:22.040
And again, this takes
quite a lot of computation.

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:28.580
And again, they used the cloud
ML Engine GPU to accelerate.

00:16:28.580 --> 00:16:32.660
And they got 10x speed
up and cost performance.

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:36.910
And the important piece here
is the cost performance,

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.880
which means basically for
the same amount of dollar,

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.290
they got 10x results.

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:45.220
So when GPUs work
for your model,

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.750
it is not only very fast.

00:16:47.750 --> 00:16:54.240
Also, it's a very much
cheaper solution to use.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:59.060
And as a result, they
created this visualization

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:01.340
of whole fishing activities.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:05.300
Each dot represents fishing
happening across the ocean.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:09.200
And as you can see, the
activity is happening

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.089
across a very large area.

00:17:12.089 --> 00:17:18.050
And also, there's very
interesting patterns happening

00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:20.750
in different time windows.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:28.630
So that is a very cool use case
of getting insights at scale.

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:33.730
So now we're going to
talk about SparkCognition,

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:41.220
who created a ML-based malware
detection service for Android.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:45.600
So I'd like to invite Bryan
Lares from SparkCognition

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.162
to share that story with us.

00:17:48.162 --> 00:17:48.870
BRYAN LARES: Hey.

00:17:48.870 --> 00:17:50.560
First of all, so my
