00:26:20.040 --> 00:26:21.250
So here's the architecture.

00:26:21.250 --> 00:26:23.590
Here's how we've leveraged
the Google ML platform.

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:28.360
First, obviously, on the
Google ML platform side,

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:30.940
for model training,
we've collected--

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:34.450
the key for malware detection is
collecting a huge, broad sample

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.790
set of malicious
or benign files.

00:26:36.790 --> 00:26:38.170
In the case of
our Android model,

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:43.300
we've collected over 100,000
malicious and benign files

00:26:43.300 --> 00:26:46.300
of a very broad sample set.

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:48.010
Then we actually have--

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:51.700
we've created three
different feature extractors

00:26:51.700 --> 00:26:54.460
to be able to
analyze [INAUDIBLE]

00:26:54.460 --> 00:26:56.890
three different feature
vectors to analyze

00:26:56.890 --> 00:26:58.930
different characteristics
within the files.

00:26:58.930 --> 00:27:04.150
Each file can literally
have over 1,000 or thousands

00:27:04.150 --> 00:27:05.890
of different
characteristics that we've

00:27:05.890 --> 00:27:07.850
put through the
machine learning model.

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:09.880
We then leveraged the
power of TensorFlow

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:13.360
to develop our deep
neural network classifier.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.290
And finally, we deploy that
on the Google ML engine

00:27:17.290 --> 00:27:20.170
to do online prediction.

00:27:20.170 --> 00:27:23.260
So then I think the second
stage of that is actually

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:27.890
what happens from your device
to actually detect the malware.

00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:30.520
So it obviously all starts
with a very small endpoint

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:32.470
agent on your mobile device.

00:27:32.470 --> 00:27:36.250
The DeepArmor agent does
file monitoring and feature

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:37.240
extraction.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.790
It then interacts with
our cloud orchestration

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:43.520
layer within our
management console

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:46.000
and to actually do
threat submission.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:49.210
We then do ingestion
through data proc and data

00:27:49.210 --> 00:27:50.380
vectorization.

00:27:50.380 --> 00:27:52.750
And then it goes through
the Google ML platform

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:56.110
with the ML Prediction
API for classification,

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:59.200
finally going back to our
DeepArmor cloud orchestration

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:01.270
layer and to our
management console,

00:28:01.270 --> 00:28:05.230
and then finally to the
DeepArmor agent for execution,

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:08.200
blocking and alerting.

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:12.970
This whole process can
take less than a second

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:17.500
to actually go from
identifying a new file

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:21.940
on a system to sending it out to
our cloud layer and the Google

00:28:21.940 --> 00:28:25.330
ML platform and coming back
with a prediction in less

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:28.300
than a second, which is
obviously critical for the user

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:28.970
experience.

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:39.620
So finally, there's
three major benefits

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:43.610
to applying machine learning
to malware detection.

00:28:43.610 --> 00:28:47.600
First of all-- and this is
by far the most important--

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:52.010
we're seeing detection rates
four times more effective

00:28:52.010 --> 00:28:54.530
versus zero-day threats
and polymorphic threats

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:57.890
compared to traditional
cybersecurity solutions.

00:28:57.890 --> 00:29:01.110
So traditional
solutions sometimes

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:05.000
that are based on
signatures see less than 20%

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:07.910
efficacy for new zero-day
threats and new hacking

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:09.290
techniques.

00:29:09.290 --> 00:29:12.320
So machine learning's making a
significant difference in that.

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:13.220
Second is speed.

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.080
As I mentioned,
our model combined

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:18.620
with the cloud ML Engine
is optimized to provide

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.390
subsecond threat analysis.

00:29:20.390 --> 00:29:21.830
And finally, scalability.

00:29:21.830 --> 00:29:25.070
Our load profile device agent
combined with the scalability

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:29.480
of the Google ML Engine makes it
possible to apply this approach

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:33.230
to millions of mobile
and IOT devices.

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:36.530
As an example from the
efficacy standpoint,

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:40.610
as we were developing this
model, with our first feature

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:43.580
set, which was strings,
we were able to reach

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:49.820
efficacy rates of greater than
90% on malware and clean files.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:52.820
When we added in
our API feature sets

00:29:52.820 --> 00:29:54.890
and our permissions
feature sets,

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:58.010
we're starting to see
results of over 99%

00:29:58.010 --> 00:30:00.860
efficacy for both
malicious and benign files.

00:30:00.860 --> 00:30:05.960
So a significant step forward
versus the traditional

00:30:05.960 --> 00:30:08.660
cybersecurity solutions.

00:30:08.660 --> 00:30:11.560
So our goal at
SparkCognition is to use

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:13.720
the power of
artificial intelligence

00:30:13.720 --> 00:30:16.870
to provide more effective
and more cost-efficient

00:30:16.870 --> 00:30:19.380
security to all
types of devices.

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:22.030
And we think that this
cloud-based model, partnering

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:25.810
with Google, can be
scaled to all kinds

00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:28.090
of different
solutions from mobile

00:30:28.090 --> 00:30:32.084
to IoT to servers to clients.

00:30:32.084 --> 00:30:33.545
So thank you.

